    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(87, 58), (53, 59), (19, 86), (61, 36), (68, 75)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 43.0, 25.0, 30.0]\n    generator_capacities = [74.0, 70.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 800  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 500  # kV\n    line_capacity = 150  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 80245  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 66  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "The utility is planning a new transmission network to connect two generator sites to three consumer load centers across five nodes. The mapped locations for these nodes are provided in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (87, 58)\n  - N1 (generator): (53, 59)\n  - N2 (consumer): (19, 86)\n  - N3 (consumer): (61, 36)\n  - N4 (consumer): (68, 75), and the functional roles (generator vs. consumer) are defined in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. The consumer demand levels are listed in Consumer demands: N2: 43.0 MW, N3: 25.0 MW, N4: 30.0 MW, and the generator output limits are specified in Generator capacities: N0: 74.0 MW, N1: 70.0 MW. Our planning decision is which transmission corridors to construct between these nodes so that every consumer is served, generators stay within their limits, and the network remains reliable.\n\nPower can only flow where we build a line. Each line we choose to construct must be able to carry the scheduled flow while respecting the maximum rating in 150 MW. Construction cost is distance-based; longer routes are more expensive, with cost per kilometer given by $80,245/km. Distances will be inferred from the node locations, so routing choices directly influence both cost and electrical performance.\n\nWe will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the planned conductor cross-sectional area is 800 mm\u00b2. For any candidate span, the resistance increases with physical length and decreases with conductor thickness. In other words, longer and thinner lines have higher resistance; shorter and thicker lines have lower resistance.\n\nOperationally, resistive losses show up as heating and follow a square law with respect to power flow. Doubling the power on a given line leads to four times the heating cost. This behavior arises because, at the operating voltage 500 kV, the current needed to move a given amount of power is proportional to power divided by voltage, and the I^2R relationship makes losses scale with the square of that current. In shorthand, the loss on a line is proportional to its resistance times (power/voltage)^2. Higher voltage reduces current, which cuts losses with a 1/V^2 effect.\n\nBecause this is a planning model, we treat losses as a financial penalty rather than deducting energy from nodal balances. Power is conserved at the nodes: the flows we schedule into each consumer must meet their demand, and the flows out of each generator must stay within its capability. Separately, we pay for the resistive heating at $66/MWh. This means the operational cost on a line is not a simple sum of flows; it increases quadratically with power flow according to the physics-based square law. That has practical implications: heavily loading a single corridor can become disproportionately expensive, while distributing flows over lower-resistance paths and using higher voltage materially reduces loss costs.\n\nThe goal is to minimize total cost\u2014the sum of construction expenditures for the lines we decide to build and the ongoing cost of resistive losses. To achieve this, the network design must ensure every consumer\u2019s demand is met, generator outputs remain within their limits, flows occur only on built lines, each line stays within 150 MW, and the square-law behavior of resistive losses is accurately captured. This approach should yield a topology that balances route length, conductor sizing, and loading so the utility meets demand reliably at the lowest combined construction and heating cost.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning approach for a loss-aware transmission build-out connecting generators to consumers\n\nWe are preparing a transmission expansion decision that will connect a set of generator and consumer nodes distributed across our service area. The locations of these nodes are specified in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and each node\u2019s role\u2014generator or consumer\u2014is defined in {NODE_TYPES}. Consumer demand obligations are given in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and generator maximum outputs are provided in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}. The planning task is to determine which transmission lines to build between these points and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met while keeping both capital and operational costs under control.\n\nThe capital side of the decision is driven by distance. Lines we do not build cannot carry power; lines we do build incur a construction cost proportional to their length, using the per\u2011kilometer rate in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. Candidate line lengths will be derived from the map coordinates, and the chosen topology must enable power to move from the generator nodes to the consumer nodes without exceeding technical limits.\n\nOperationally, we need to account for resistive losses\u2014the I^2R heating that occurs when current flows through a conductor. These losses are not a linear function of power flow. They follow a square law: doubling the power flow through a line quadruples the resistive losses. Because current is power divided by voltage, higher voltage reduces current and therefore dramatically reduces these heating losses. Our conductors are copper, and the wire material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}. The resistance of each candidate line segment depends on its length and the conductor size; the conductor cross\u2011section is specified in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Taken together, resistance increases with distance and decreases with thicker conductors. We monetize these physics in planning by applying an operational loss cost based on the physical relationship Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power / Voltage)^2, with the cost rate in {LOSS_COST_RATE} and the system operating voltage in {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. This keeps our model faithful to the real behavior of losses and avoids the pitfalls of linear approximations.\n\nSeveral network constraints must be respected:\n- Power can only flow on corridors we choose to build.\n- Each consumer must receive sufficient power to meet its demand.\n- Generator outputs cannot exceed their maximums.\n- Each built line has a maximum thermal rating, and power flow on any line must not exceed {LINE_CAPACITY}.\n- For planning purposes, we treat power flow as conserved at the nodes: nodal balances are enforced without deducting physical losses from the delivered energy. Losses are accounted for as a financial penalty rather than a subtraction in the mass balance.\n\nThis model captures a fundamental trade-off:\n- Building longer or additional lines increases capital cost, as determined by distance and {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}.\n- Concentrating large flows on a single corridor may appear cheaper to build but can be expensive to operate because resistive losses grow with the square of the flow.\n- Splitting flows across multiple paths, using thicker conductors, or leveraging our operating voltage can reduce heating costs, because the loss cost scales as Resistance \u00d7 (Power / Voltage)^2 and carries a multiplier from {LOSS_COST_RATE}.\n- However, as we spread flows or increase lengths, construction costs may rise, and line capacity constraints must be respected.\n\nOur objective is to select which lines to construct and determine the power routing on those lines to minimize total cost: the sum of construction expenditures and the quadratic loss costs associated with resistive heating. We will avoid any approach that simply sums flows or applies linear loss factors; instead, we will use the square\u2011law loss model so the solution reflects the reality that doubling the power flow leads to four times the heating cost. The nodal power balance will remain strictly lossless, and losses will be treated as an external financial penalty. This ensures we find a network configuration that both meets all demands and sensibly balances capital spending against the operational costs driven by I^2R physics."
}